(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-dermatology-pathophys-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which receptors does the lecture name for the sensations of touch and pressure?","choices":["Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles","Langerhans cells and melanocytes","Meissner's corpuscles, Pacinian corpuscles and Merkel cells","Ruffini endings, Krause bulbs and free nerve endings only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meissner's serve fine discriminative touch and low-frequency vibration, Pacinian respond to deep pressure and vibration, and Merkel cells serve light touch. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the two phases of allergic contact dermatitis?","choices":["Irritation, then hyperkeratosis of the stratum corneum","Radial growth, then vertical growth","Sensitization, in which Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells to create specialised memory; and elicitation, in which re-exposure to the same hapten promotes T cell migration and cutaneous inflammation","Sensitization by mast cell degranulation, followed by elicitation through the release of histamine into the surrounding dermal tissue on every subsequent exposure to that same allergen thereafter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a delayed hypersensitivity reaction occurring 48 to 72 hours after exposure. Nickel and poison ivy are the examples. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the main pathologic finding in nail clubbing, and what mediates it?","choices":["Incomplete keratinization at the nail bed","Increased capillary density, with increased release of vascular endothelial growth factor","Overgrowth of the connective tissue within the nail bed itself over a period of time","A halt of keratin production at the matrix"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is associated with lung, cardiovascular, liver and inflammatory bowel disease. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which cells drive psoriasis, and which cytokines does the lecture name?","choices":["Melanocytes proliferate at the dermal-epidermal junction","Active T cells infiltrating the epidermis stimulate keratinocyte proliferation and excessive cytokine production, particularly tumour necrosis factor alpha, interferon gamma and interleukin-12","Mast cells release histamine which drives keratinocyte proliferation","Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells, producing a delayed hypersensitivity reaction 48 to 72 hours after each exposure to the allergen in question every single time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a chronic autoimmune inflammatory dermatosis, and skin cells multiply up to ten times faster than normal. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which factors contribute to seborrhoeic dermatitis, and where does it occur?","choices":["Cutaneous microbiome dysbiosis, altered immune response and epidermal barrier dysfunction, affecting regions rich in sebaceous glands — scalp, face and upper trunk","A single fungal pathogen acting alone, affecting mainly the palms of the hands and the soles of the feet rather than the sebaceous areas of the skin","Autoimmune T cell activation, affecting the elbows and knees","Ultraviolet damage, affecting sun-exposed skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture is explicit that the cause is incompletely understood. Dandruff is the non-inflammatory form. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What produces lichenification, and what changes occur in the tissue?","choices":["Loss of melanocytes across a circumscribed area","Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with accumulation of fluid between the two layers, forming a large blister that lifts the surface away","Slowing of keratinocyte division with elastin degradation","Long-term scratching or rubbing of a pruritic area in an itch-scratch cycle, producing hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis with solid, firm, thick plaques without scaling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and lichen simplex chronicus is the named condition. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which causes are given for bullae?","choices":["Capillary rupture and thrombocytopenia","Androgen stimulation of sebaceous glands","Mechanical factors, infections, toxins, chemical exposure and autoimmune reactions","Increased melanin production and cumulative ultraviolet light exposure alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with bullous impetigo as the bacterial example and bullous pemphigoid as the autoimmune one. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A patient has thick, leathery plaques on the neck and forearms with no scaling, after months of scratching. Which secondary lesion is this, mechanistically?","choices":["Ulcer, arising from focal loss of both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it","Atrophy, from slowed keratinocyte division","Lichenification, from hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis driven by the itch-scratch cycle","Scale, from desquamating stratum corneum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of scaling is what separates it from psoriasis, whose plaques do scale. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are the growth phases of melanoma, in order?","choices":["Vertical growth down into the dermis first, then radial spread within the epidermis afterwards once the tumour has thickened sufficiently to invade","Inflammatory, proliferative, then remodelling","Sensitization then elicitation","Radial growth within the epidermis, then vertical growth penetrating the dermis, then increasing tumour thickness, then metastatic invasion of lymphatics and blood vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The shift from radial to vertical is the point at which depth begins to matter. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What forms during the proliferative phase, and what is it made of?","choices":["A fibrin haemostatic plug, over days 1 to 3","Type I collagen laid down in parallel bundles, over days 21 to one year of remodelling","Keratohyalin granules within the granular layer","Granulation tissue, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells, over days 4 to 21"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those three cell types supply clearance, matrix and new vessels respectively. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the suspected mechanism of koilonychia?","choices":["Impaired keratin synthesis associated with deficiency anaemia, weakening the nail plate into raised ridges that are thin and concave","A halt of keratin production producing horizontal transverse grooves across the surface of the nail plate itself","Overgrowth of connective tissue producing an opaque nail","Incomplete keratinization producing white spots"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture notes the exact pathophysiology is unknown. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which epidermal layer forms the semi-impermeable barrier, and what is it made of?","choices":["The papillary dermis, a thin superficial connective tissue layer","The stratum corneum, 20 to 30 layers of keratin and dead keratinocytes cemented together","The stratum basale, a single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes at the base","The stratum granulosum, 3 to 5 layers of diamond-shaped cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That packing is what makes callused skin, and losing it is what makes barrier-defect conditions possible. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which protein deficiency contributes to atopic dermatitis, and what does it reflect?","choices":["Insufficient collagen, reflecting fibroblast failure","Insufficient elastin, reflecting loss of recoil","Insufficient keratohyalin, reflecting loss of the granular layer","Insufficient filaggrin, reflecting a compromised epidermal barrier"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside an overactive immune system and genetic, environmental and psychogenic factors. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of urticaria?","choices":["T cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity 48 to 72 hours after exposure","Dysplastic keratinocyte proliferation from ultraviolet damage","Capillary rupture with extravasation of blood","Mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, producing wheals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it may be acute or chronic with a wide range of triggers. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What gives the spinous layer its alternative name of prickle cell layer?","choices":["Keratohyalin granules that give the cells a rough and irregular outline when viewed on microscopy","Melanin granules transferred from melanocytes","Dead flattened keratinocytes packed into scales","Keratinocytes connected by intercellular connections called desmosomes, which form a spiny appearance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This layer also contains the dendritic and Langerhans cells that act as immune sentinels. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does an epidermal inclusion cyst arise from and contain?","choices":["A sebaceous gland, and it contains only squalene","A blood vessel, and it contains clotted blood","A hair follicle, and it contains keratin","A sweat gland, and it contains sodium and chloride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cyst is an elevated, circumscribed, encapsulated lesion in the dermis or subcutaneous layer with liquid or semisolid contents. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How do dermatophytes obtain nutrition, and in which layer do they typically live?","choices":["They proliferate within the dermis, digesting collagen","Spores secrete keratinases and proteases to metabolise and digest keratin in skin, hair and nails, typically within the stratum corneum","They invade epidermal basal cells through microabrasions and drive proliferation of the epidermis above them over time","They release histamine from mast cells producing wheals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — Trichophyton, Microsporum and Epidermophyton are the three most common, and the annular patch with central clearing follows from this. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How do eccrine and apocrine sweat glands differ in secretion and function?","choices":["Eccrine secrete keratin and apocrine secrete melanin","Eccrine open onto the skin surface and secrete water and electrolytes for cooling by evaporation; apocrine sit mainly in the axilla and anogenital areas and secrete protein and fatty lipids, functioning as scent glands","Eccrine glands sit in the axilla and anogenital areas and secrete protein and fatty lipids; apocrine glands open onto the surface of the skin and secrete water and electrolytes for cooling the body","Both secrete sebum, differing only in the body regions they occupy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Body odour comes from bacterial degradation of apocrine sweat, not from the sweat itself. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Where do melanocytes normally sit, and what drives their malignant transformation?","choices":["Within the stratum corneum, transformed by keratinase digestion","In the reticular layer of the dermis, transformed by dysregulation of the fibroblasts that reside within it over a long period of time","In the basal layer, transformed by Hedgehog pathway activation","At the dermal-epidermal junction, with ultraviolet light and oxidative stress damaging melanocyte DNA and leading to uninhibited growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the risk is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait with variable penetrance. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How is melanoma risk inherited, according to the lecture?","choices":["As an autosomal dominant trait with variable penetrance","As an autosomal recessive trait with complete penetrance","As an X-linked trait","It is entirely environmental with no inherited component"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, though ultraviolet light and oxidative stress remain the primary driving factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A patient reacts to a nickel earring 48 to 72 hours after wearing it. Which mechanism does the delay reflect?","choices":["Viral proliferation within basal keratinocytes","Delayed hypersensitivity requiring T cell migration after re-exposure to a hapten previously presented by Langerhans cells","An immediate immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation occurring upon the very first contact with the metal","Direct chemical injury requiring no prior exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The delay is the time the cellular response takes, which is what distinguishes it from an immediate mast cell reaction. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What causes Beau's lines, and what are they associated with?","choices":["Increased capillary density, associated with lung disease","Impaired keratin synthesis, associated with deficiency anaemia","A halt of keratin production producing horizontal or transverse grooves, associated with illness, stress, injury to the nail and malnourishment","Overgrowth of connective tissue within the nail bed, associated with chronic liver disease and with diabetes mellitus in particular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the groove marks the period during which the nail stopped being made. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which skin cancer is described as the most common malignancy in humans, and how does it behave?","choices":["Actinic keratosis, which is by far the most common malignancy occurring in human beings","Basal cell carcinoma, slow growing and rarely metastasising, typically on the head and neck","Squamous cell carcinoma, rapidly metastasising","Melanoma, slow growing and rarely metastasising"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What produces Terry's nails, and what conditions is it associated with?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed, associated with aging, liver disease, congestive heart failure or diabetes","Increased capillary density together with vascular endothelial growth factor release into the tissue locally over time","Impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anaemia","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the nail is opaque except for a dark band at the tip. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Why does an ulcer scar while an erosion generally does not?","choices":["An ulcer destroys collagen in the dermis, so healing must lay down new collagen; an erosion spares the dermal-epidermal junction and the dermis beneath it","An ulcer is infected and an erosion is sterile","An ulcer is larger, and size alone determines scarring","An ulcer involves a loss of melanocytes from the basal layer which cannot be reversed once it has occurred in the tissue at any point afterwards"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Scarring is a dermal repair process, so a lesion confined above the junction has nothing to scar. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the tissue basis of a papule?","choices":["Separation of epidermis from dermis with fluid accumulation","Focal loss of the epidermis that does not penetrate below the dermal-epidermal junction beneath it","Hyperplasia of the epidermis with hyperkeratosis, plus inflammation and dense collagen fibres in the dermis","Fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or beneath the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and seborrhoeic keratosis is the example given. A papule is elevated, solid, and under 5 millimetres. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the primary component of the dermis, which cell produces it, and what does it provide?","choices":["Collagen, produced by fibroblasts, providing tensile strength","Keratin, produced by keratinocytes, providing an impermeable barrier","Elastin, produced by mast cells, providing tensile strength","Melanin, produced by melanocytes, providing ultraviolet protection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with elastic fibres of elastin and fibrillin providing elastic recoil and flexibility alongside it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How often does the epidermis turn over, and what modifies that rate?","choices":["Turnover ceases after adolescence","Every 30 to 60 days, and turnover is more rapid in younger patients","Every 24 to 48 hours, with no age dependence","Every 6 to 12 months, and considerably more rapid in older patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age-dependent turnover matters because it sets how quickly the epidermis can repair itself. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of a hypopigmented macule such as vitiligo, against a hyperpigmented one?","choices":["Loss of melanocytes, against increased melanin production in the basal layer of the epidermis","Increased melanin production within the epidermis, as against loss of melanocytes entirely","Capillary rupture with blood deposition in the dermis","Dilatation and thinning of superficial dermal vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ephelides, café au lait spots and Mongolian spots are the hyperpigmented examples given. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the tissue distinction between an erosion and an ulcer?","choices":["An erosion is focal loss of epidermis that does not penetrate below the dermal-epidermal junction; an ulcer is focal loss of epidermis AND dermis, with destruction of collagen and infiltration of inflammatory cells","An erosion involves both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it; an ulcer is limited to the epidermis alone and spares the dermal-epidermal junction entirely, so it never destroys collagen at all","An erosion is fluid-filled and an ulcer is solid","An erosion is a primary lesion and an ulcer is a secondary lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The junction is the boundary that separates them, and crossing it is what brings collagen destruction into play. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
